<div style="border: 2px solid #1a5276; padding: 15px; text-align: center; background-color: #e0f2f7;">
    <h1 style="color: #1a5276; margin: 0;">Lesson Planner – Theme # <span style="font-weight: normal;">What was Your Previous School Like?</span> – Lesson # 5</h1>
    <p style="color: #1a5276; margin: 5px 0 0 0; font-size: 1.1em;"><i>Complete this planner five times per theme, once per lesson (Lesson 1 through Lesson 5).</i></p>
</div>

<table style="border: 1px dashed #333; border-collapse: collapse; width: 100%; margin: 10px 0;">
    <tr>
        <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;"><p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Lesson #:</b> 5</p></td>
        <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;"><p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Skills Focus:</b> Mediation</p></td>
    </tr>
    <tr>
        <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;"><p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Grade:</b> 7th Grade (CEFR: A2.2)</p></td>
        <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;"><p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Scenario:</b> Our New Classmates</p></td>
        <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;"><p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Theme:</b> What was Your Previous School Like?</p></td>
    </tr>
    <tr>
        <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;"><p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Date(s):</b> From ___ to ___</p></td>
        <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;" colspan="2"><p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Learning Sequence Time:</b> 45–50 min</p></td>
    </tr>
    <tr>
        <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;" colspan="3">
            <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Specific Objective:</b> Mediation: Text: Simplify messages for peers. Text: Summarize a classmate's presentation for a teacher.</p>
        </td>
    </tr>
    <tr>
        <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;" colspan="3">
            <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Learning Outcome:</b> Mediation: Can simplify messages for peers about school events or activities (e.g., "Victoria said the event will take place at my previous school." "The activity is to learn more about our new classmate Adrian."). Can summarize a classmate's presentation for a teacher (e.g., "Oscar talked about his old school. He said it had a big playground and that he loved math class."). Can explain classroom rules to a new student using simple language (e.g., "We can't use our phones during class, but Teacher Angie said we can use them at lunch.").</p>
        </td>
    </tr>
</table>

<h2 style="color: #1a5276; text-align: center;">The Six Action-Oriented Approach (AOA) Lesson Stages</h2>
<table style="border: 1px dashed #333; border-collapse: collapse; width: 100%; margin: 10px 0;">
    <thead>
        <tr>
            <th style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px; background-color: #f2f2f2; color: #1a5276; width: 30%;">Stage & Description</th>
            <th style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px; background-color: #f2f2f2; color: #1a5276; width: 50%;">Activities & Teacher Actions</th>
            <th style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px; background-color: #f2f2f2; color: #1a5276; width: 20%;">Estimated Date and Time</th>
        </tr>
    </thead>
    <tbody>
        <tr>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;"><p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>STAGE 1 — Warm-up / Pre-task</b><br>Engagement, Modeling and Clarification</p></td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Warm-up:</b> Students work in pairs. The teacher displays a simple image of a school event (e.g., "school fair," "sports day"). One student describes the event in 2-3 sentences, then the other student has to simplify that description into one shorter sentence for a "new classmate." For example, Student A: "Last year, our school had a big sports day with many games and races." Student B (simplifying): "Our school had sports games."</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Modeling:</b> The teacher explains and models mediation using a sentence related to the 21st Century Skills Project. "Student A said, 'My old school was very far from my house, so I always took the bus, but my new school is closer.' I can simplify this for a new student by saying, 'Student A's old school was far, but this new school is closer.' I can also summarize it for the principal: 'Student A compared travel to his old and new school.'" The teacher emphasizes using simpler words and focusing on key information.</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Clarification (CCQs):</b>
                    <ul style="list-style-type: disc; margin-left: 20px; padding: 0;">
                        <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">What does it mean to "simplify" a message? (Expected answer: Use fewer words, easier words, or shorter sentences.)</li>
                        <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">When do we "summarize" something? (Expected answer: When we want to give the main idea quickly.)</li>
                    </ul>
                </p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;"><p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">___ min</p></td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;"><p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>STAGE 2 — Presentation</b><br>Input of new language/content connected to Scenario: "Our New Classmates"</p></td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Material:</b> A short, fictional "Welcome Message for a New Student" from the school principal or a teacher, which includes some school rules or important information (e.g., "Welcome! Our school community is friendly. Recess is at 10:00 AM. We usually have exciting activities in the library after lunch. Please remember, phones are not allowed during classes."). The message uses Present Simple and some basic future tense.</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Communicative Competence:</b> Present personal and classmate information in group discussions using appropriate language. Participate in guided discussions about preferences and routines. Respond to questions about classmates and schools.</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Comprehension tasks:</b> Students read the "Welcome Message."
                    <ul style="list-style-type: disc; margin-left: 20px; padding: 0;">
                        <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>Gist question:</b> What is the main purpose of this message? (To welcome a new student and give information.)</li>
                        <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>True/False:</b> Read the message and decide if the following sentences are True or False.
                            <ul style="list-style-type: circle; margin-left: 20px; padding: 0;">
                                <li style="margin-bottom: 2px; text-align: justify;">The school has a quiet community. (False - it says friendly)</li>
                                <li style="margin-bottom: 2px; text-align: justify;">Phones are okay during classes. (False)</li>
                            </ul>
                        </li>
                    </ul>
                </p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;"><p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">___ min</p></td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;"><p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>STAGE 3 — Preparation / Practice</b><br>Accuracy focus on Mediation</p></td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Communicative Skill & Content:</b> Simplifying messages for peers, summarizing information. Using Present/Past Simple, Comparatives/Superlatives (e.g., "bigger than," "the most interesting"), Future tense. Vocabulary: classmate, school, activity, rule, schedule, library, recess, playground, friend, teacher, previous, new. Pronunciation practice: verb endings /s/, /z/, /ɪz/ (e.g., "walks," "reads," "teaches").</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Detailed Procedure:</b>
                    <ul style="list-style-type: disc; margin-left: 20px; padding: 0;">
                        <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>1. Controlled Practice (Simplify the Rules):</b> Students receive sentences from the "Welcome Message" and other simple classroom rules. They rewrite them in simpler language for a "new student." Teacher models pronunciation of plurals and 3rd person singular verbs like "teachers" /ɪz/, "books" /s/, "friends" /z/.
                            <ul>
                                <li style="margin-bottom: 2px; text-align: justify;">"Phones are not permitted during instructional hours." → "You can't use phones in class."</li>
                                <li style="margin-bottom: 2px; text-align: justify;">"All students are encouraged to actively participate in extracurricular activities." → "Try to join school activities."</li>
                            </ul>
                        </li>
                        <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>2. Semi-controlled (Summarize a Classmate's Project Idea):</b> Students are in pairs. Student A describes an idea for their "My Previous School vs. My New School" digital poster (e.g., "My old school had a small playground, but this school has a much bigger one, and we often play soccer there. We also had music class every Friday in my old school."). Student B then summarizes this information in one sentence for the teacher. E.g., "Student A said his new school's playground is bigger than his old one, and they play soccer. His old school used to have music classes."</li>
                        <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>3. Pair Interaction (Peer Mediation Check):</b> Students form new pairs. Student A shares 2-3 facts from their project draft (e.g., "My previous school was in the countryside. It was small but friendly. We will have a school trip next month here."). Student B listens, then summarizes these facts for an imaginary "new teacher" in 1-2 sentences. They switch roles. Teacher circulates, providing feedback on clarity and use of comparatives ("bigger," "smaller") and past/present simple.</li>
                    </ul>
                </p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;"><p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">___ min</p></td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;"><p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>STAGE 4 — Performance / Production</b><br>Communicative task where students USE the language in a realistic scenario: "Our New Classmates"</p></td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">Students work in their project groups to finalize their "My Previous School vs. My New School" digital poster. Each group will present their poster, with a specific mediation task.</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Task Format:</b> Group Presentation with Mediation Role-Play.
                    <ul style="list-style-type: disc; margin-left: 20px; padding: 0;">
                        <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">One student from the group presents their digital poster, highlighting 2-3 key comparisons using comparatives and superlatives (e.g., "My old school was smaller, but this one has the biggest library.").</li>
                        <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">A second student from the same group acts as the "mediator." This student must then simplify one of the comparisons for a "new classmate" (played by another student or the teacher) using simpler language. For example, if the presenter said, "My previous school had more stringent uniform regulations," the mediator simplifies, "At my old school, uniforms were more strict." They also summarize the whole presentation for the "teacher" (real teacher) in 1-2 sentences.</li>
                        <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">The focus is on mediating information about their school experience and the project to different audiences.</li>
                    </ul>
                </p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Connection to Theme/Scenario:</b> The activity directly uses the "My Previous School vs. My New School" project. Students mediate information about their schools and classmates to new members of the "Our New Classmates" scenario.</p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;"><p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">___ min</p></td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;"><p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>STAGE 5 — Assessment / Post-task</b><br>Formative assessment strategy</p></td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Formative Assessment Strategy:</b> Teacher observation checklist focusing on mediation skills, supported by a simple rubric for the project presentation and mediation task.</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Assessment Task:</b> Each group performs their Stage 4 presentation and mediation role-play. The teacher assesses the designated "mediator" student on their ability to simplify messages for peers and summarize the presentation for the teacher.</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Evaluation Criteria (Mediator Focus):</b>
                    <ul style="list-style-type: disc; margin-left: 20px; padding: 0;">
                        <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>Clarity and Simplicity:</b> Simplifies messages for peers using appropriate A2.2 vocabulary and grammar (Present/Past Simple, Comparatives).</li>
                        <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>Summarization:</b> Accurately summarizes classmate's presentation for the teacher, capturing key points.</li>
                        <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>Target Language Use:</b> Integrates vocabulary (school, playground, library, activities, previous, new) and grammar (comparatives, used to) effectively.</li>
                        <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>Pronunciation:</b> Intelligible pronunciation, with attention to /s/, /z/, /ɪz/ sounds in plural nouns and 3rd person singular verbs.</li>
                        <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>Collaboration:</b> Works effectively with group members during the presentation.</li>
                    </ul>
                </p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Student Self-Assessment / Exit Ticket:</b> Students complete an exit ticket with the following prompts:
                    <ul style="list-style-type: disc; margin-left: 20px; padding: 0;">
                        <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Did I simplify my group's message clearly for the "new classmate"? Yes / No / A little</li>
                        <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Was I able to summarize my classmate's presentation well for the teacher? Yes / No / A little</li>
                    </ul>
                </p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;"><p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">___ min</p></td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;"><p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>STAGE 6 — Reflection</b></p></td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Student self-reflection:</b>
                    <ul style="list-style-type: disc; margin-left: 20px; padding: 0;">
                        <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">What was challenging about simplifying or summarizing information about our project?</li>
                        <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">How can mediating information help new students or friends understand things better?</li>
                    </ul>
                </p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Teacher reflection:</b> The mediation activities allowed students to apply their knowledge of school comparisons in a practical, communicative way. The project-based learning effectively integrated all skills. The role-play aspect in Stage 4 and 5 was engaging. Some students found it challenging to consistently simplify without losing key information; more practice on identifying essential details would be beneficial. Next time, I could provide sentence frames specifically for simplifying rules for a new student. This lesson effectively concluded the "What was Your Previous School Like?" theme, bringing together grammar, vocabulary, and all four skills, culminating in the mediation of their final project.</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Connection to next lesson in the theme sequence:</b> This lesson concludes the theme unit. The next unit will introduce a new theme and set of linguistic objectives, building on the communicative skills developed here, especially in summarizing and explaining.</p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;"><p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">___ min</p></td>
        </tr>
    </tbody>
</table>